   "answer": "Adding arbitrarily many processors fails to deliver unlimited speedup for a fixed-size program because of the **serial fraction** of the code. According to the lecture, even a small serial fraction caps the total speedup when the problem size is fixed [S1, p. 66].\n\n**Reasoning and Calculation:**\nThis limitation is described by **Amdahl's Law**, which is given by the formula:\n\n$$S(p) = \\frac{1}{(1 - f) + \\frac{f}{p}}$$\n\nWhere:\n*   $S(p)$ is the speedup.\n*   $p$ is the number of processors/threads.\n*   $f$ is the parallelizable fraction.\n*   $1 - f$ is the serial fraction [S1, p. 66].\n\nAs the number of processors ($p$) approaches infinity, the term $\\frac{f}{p}$ approaches 0. The speedup then approaches:\n\n$$S(\\infty) = \\frac{1}{1 - f}$$\n\nThis means the maximum possible speedup is determined entirely by the serial portion of the program. The lecture provides numerical intuition for this:\n*   If the parallelizable fraction $f = 0.90$ (meaning 10% is serial), the maximum speedup is capped at **10x**.\n*   If the parallelizable fraction $f = 0.99$ (meaning 1% is serial), the maximum speedup is capped at **100x** [S1, p. 66].\n\nTherefore, no matter how many processors are added, the serial part of the program remains and prevents the speedup from becoming infinite.",
